<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferenc Nagy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferenc Nagy was a leader of the Independent Smallholders' Party who served as Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from February 1946 to May 1947, during the short-lived Second Hungarian Republic. The Communists, backed by the Soviet occupation, broke up his party through arrests and accusations of conspiracy. While on holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> he was forced to resign, and he spent the rest of his life in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferenc-nagy.jpg" alt="Ferenc Nagy" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arlen (MTI photo) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagy_Ferenc-MTI_1946.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferenc Nagy was born on 8 October 1903 into a family of middle-level peasants in the small town of Bisse. Self-taught, he began in politics by writing news articles, co-founded the Peasant Union and in October 1930 took part in founding the Independent Smallholders' Party. He entered Parliament in 1939 and opposed the war and the Nazis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> arrested him after the German occupation of March 1944, but the government of Géza Lakatos had him released, and after the Arrow Cross coup he went into hiding.</p><p>After the war he helped organise the provisional government and became second-in-command of his party after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoltan-tildy">Zoltán Tildy</a>. When the Smallholders won a large majority in November 1945, pressure from the Soviet-dominated Allied Commission forced a coalition with the Communists and Social Democrats, and Nagy became speaker of parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As prime minister Nagy favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> over both the prewar aristocratic order and a Communist dictatorship. His government ended a world-record hyperinflation by replacing the pengő with the forint in August 1946, helped by gold reserves that President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry Truman</a> agreed in June 1946 to return. It signed the Paris Peace Treaties of 1947 and a population exchange with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p><p>The Communists used salami tactics against his party, starting with a Left Bloc in March 1946 that demanded the expulsion of 20 Smallholder deputies. Nagy hoped to appease them until a peace treaty brought a Soviet withdrawal. In 1947 the party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi">Mátyás Rákosi</a> alleged a vast conspiracy, forced the arrest or recall of over 50 Smallholder deputies, and the Soviets abducted the party's general secretary, Béla Kovács. Nagy left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> on 14 May 1947; with his son held hostage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, he agreed to resign on 30 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Granted asylum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Nagy wrote The Struggle behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a> (1948) and bought a farm in Herndon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, with its royalties. He sat on the Hungarian Committee in exile, attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of 1955 as an observer for the United States government, and was kept from returning in 1956 by the authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He led the International Peasant Union from 1964 to 1970 and died at Herndon on 12 June 1979. He was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s last non-Communist head of government until 1989–90.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferenc Nagy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The parliament abolished the monarchy of the Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) and proclaimed a republic on 1 February 1946, with the Smallholder Zoltán Tildy as president and <b>Ferenc Nagy</b> as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-hungarian-republic">Second Hungarian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bisse, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Fairfax, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.91,18.26&amp;year=1952" aria-label="Bisse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4982 547 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferenc Nagy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5432 817h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5432 817h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.91,18.26&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
